    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-14g",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "14G Relationship of Division to other laws",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-14g"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf, p.42",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The power of arrest conferred by section 14A on protective service\nofficers is in addition to, and not in derogation of, powers of arrest\navailable to protective service officers under any other law of the\nCommonwealth or the law of a State or Territory.\n(2) Subject to subsection (3), where a protective service officer, in the\ncourse of his or her duties as a protective service officer, arrests a\nperson for any offence (whether the arrest is made under\nsection 14A, under any other law of the Commonwealth or under\nthe law of a State or Territory), sections 14B to 14F apply in\nrelation to the arrest despite any other law of the Commonwealth\nor the law of a State or Territory.\n(3) Subsection (2) has effect subject to sections 252 and 253 of the\nMigration Act 1958.\n(4) Subject to subsection (2), the powers conferred, and duties\nimposed, by this Division on protective service officers are in\n\n\nConstitution, functions and powers of the Australian Federal Police  Part II\nPowers and duties of protective service officers  Division 3\nSection 14G\nAustralian Federal Police Act 1979\nCompilation date: 08/11/2025\naddition to, and not in derogation of, any other powers conferred,\nor duties imposed, by any other law of the Commonwealth or the\nlaw of a State or Territory, and this Division is not intended to\nexclude or limit the operation of any other law of the\nCommonwealth or the law of a State or Territory providing for\nsuch powers or duties in so far as it is capable of operating\nconcurrently with this Division.\n(5) This Division, in so far as it protects the individual, is in addition\nto, and not in derogation of, any rights and freedoms of the\nindividual, whether under the law of the Commonwealth or of a\nState or Territory, and this Division is not intended to exclude or\nlimit the operation of any law of the Commonwealth or of a State\nor Territory providing for those rights and freedoms in so far as it\nis capable of operating concurrently with this Division.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The power of arrest conferred by section 14A on protective service\nofficers is in addition to, and not in derogation of, powers of arrest\navailable to protective service officers under any other law of the\nCommonwealth or the law of a State or Territory.\n(2) Subject to subsection (3), where a protective service officer, in the\ncourse of his or her duties as a protective service officer, arrests a\nperson for any offence (whether the arrest is made under\nsection 14A, under any other law of the Commonwealth or under\nthe law of a State or Territory), sections 14B to 14F apply in\nrelation to the arrest despite any other law of the Commonwealth\nor the law of a State or Territory.\n(3) Subsection (2) has effect subject to sections 252 and 253 of the\nMigration Act 1958.\n(4) Subject to subsection (2), the powers conferred, and duties\nimposed, by this Division on protective service officers are in\n\n\nConstitution, functions and powers of the Australian Federal Police  Part II\nPowers and duties of protective service officers  Division 3\nSection 14G\nAustralian Federal Police Act 1979\nCompilation date: 08/11/2025\naddition to, and not in derogation of, any other powers conferred,\nor duties imposed, by any other law of the Commonwealth or the\nlaw of a State or Territory, and this Division is not intended to\nexclude or limit the operation of any other law of the\nCommonwealth or the law of a State or Territory providing for\nsuch powers or duties in so far as it is capable of operating\nconcurrently with this Division.\n(5) This Division, in so far as it protects the individual, is in addition\nto, and not in derogation of, any rights and freedoms of the\nindividual, whether under the law of the Commonwealth or of a\nState or Territory, and this Division is not intended to exclude or\nlimit the operation of any law of the Commonwealth or of a State\nor Territory providing for those rights and freedoms in so far as it\nis capable of operating concurrently with this Division.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-14g-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-14g-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-14g-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-14g-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-14g-ss-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "60fd312db4503e54071e17e6f1d4c2b8d4ac50da5bb4d2fda34c87f009afaecf"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-14g",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "14G Relationship of Division to other laws",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 42,
      "values:originalText": "(1) The power of arrest conferred by section 14A on protective service\nofficers is in addition to, and not in derogation of, powers of arrest\navailable to protective service officers under any other law of the\nCommonwealth or the law of a State or Territory.\n(2) Subject to subsection (3), where a protective service officer, in the\ncourse of his or her duties as a protective service officer, arrests a\nperson for any offence (whether the arrest is made under\nsection 14A, under any other law of the Commonwealth or under\nthe law of a State or Territory), sections 14B to 14F apply in\nrelation to the arrest despite any other law of the Commonwealth\nor the law of a State or Territory.\n(3) Subsection (2) has effect subject to sections 252 and 253 of the\nMigration Act 1958.\n(4) Subject to subsection (2), the powers conferred, and duties\nimposed, by this Division on protective service officers are in\n\n\nConstitution, functions and powers of the Australian Federal Police  Part II\nPowers and duties of protective service officers  Division 3\nSection 14G\nAustralian Federal Police Act 1979\nCompilation date: 08/11/2025\naddition to, and not in derogation of, any other powers conferred,\nor duties imposed, by any other law of the Commonwealth or the\nlaw of a State or Territory, and this Division is not intended to\nexclude or limit the operation of any other law of the\nCommonwealth or the law of a State or Territory providing for\nsuch powers or duties in so far as it is capable of operating\nconcurrently with this Division.\n(5) This Division, in so far as it protects the individual, is in addition\nto, and not in derogation of, any rights and freedoms of the\nindividual, whether under the law of the Commonwealth or of a\nState or Territory, and this Division is not intended to exclude or\nlimit the operation of any law of the Commonwealth or of a State\nor Territory providing for those rights and freedoms in so far as it\nis capable of operating concurrently with this Division."
    },
