    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-14k",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "14K Seizure of things found",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-14k"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf, p.48",
      "values:originalText": "Designated person may seize a thing being searched for\n(1) If, as a result of conducting a search under section 14J, a\ndesignated person, or a person who conducted the search under\nsubsection 14J(3) on behalf of a designated person, finds the thing\nfor which the designated person or person was searching, the\ndesignated person may seize the thing.\nDesignated person may seize a dangerous thing\n(2) If, as a result of conducting a search under section 14J, a\ndesignated person, or a person who conducted the search under\nsubsection 14J(3) on behalf of a designated person, finds a weapon,\nor other thing, that the designated person suspects on reasonable\ngrounds is likely to be used by the suspect or another to cause\ndeath or serious harm to a person:\n\n\nConstitution, functions and powers of the Australian Federal Police  Part II\nPowers and duties of members, special members and protective service officers relating\nto protective service functions  Division 4\nSection 14L\nAustralian Federal Police Act 1979\nCompilation date: 08/11/2025\n(a) in respect of whom the Australian Federal Police is\nperforming protective service functions; or\n(b) who is in a place, or in the vicinity of a person, place or\nthing, in respect of which the Australian Federal Police is\nperforming protective service functions;\nthe designated person may seize the weapon or thing.\nProtective service officer must deliver thing seized to police officer\n(3) If the designated person is a protective service officer, the\ndesignated person must ensure that the thing is delivered into the\ncustody of a police officer as soon as practicable.",
      "skos:definition": "Designated person may seize a thing being searched for\n(1) If, as a result of conducting a search under section 14J, a\ndesignated person, or a person who conducted the search under\nsubsection 14J(3) on behalf of a designated person, finds the thing\nfor which the designated person or person was searching, the\ndesignated person may seize the thing.\nDesignated person may seize a dangerous thing\n(2) If, as a result of conducting a search under section 14J, a\ndesignated person, or a person who conducted the search under\nsubsection 14J(3) on behalf of a designated person, finds a weapon,\nor other thing, that the designated person suspects on reasonable\ngrounds is likely to be used by the suspect or another to cause\ndeath or serious harm to a person:\n\n\nConstitution, functions and powers of the Australian Federal Police  Part II\nPowers and duties of members, special members and protective service officers relating\nto protective service functions  Division 4\nSection 14L\nAustralian Federal Police Act 1979\nCompilation date: 08/11/2025\n(a) in respect of whom the Australian Federal Police is\nperforming protective service functions; or\n(b) who is in a place, or in the vicinity of a person, place or\nthing, in respect of which the Australian Federal Police is\nperforming protective service functions;\nthe designated person may seize the weapon or thing.\nProtective service officer must deliver thing seized to police officer\n(3) If the designated person is a protective service officer, the\ndesignated person must ensure that the thing is delivered into the\ncustody of a police officer as soon as practicable.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-14k-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-14k-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-14k-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "4a19bec0ba6e8742c77a55074ab359f2bccedcd58bbd5ac6818d31f244adad18"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-14k",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "14K Seizure of things found",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 48,
      "values:originalText": "Designated person may seize a thing being searched for\n(1) If, as a result of conducting a search under section 14J, a\ndesignated person, or a person who conducted the search under\nsubsection 14J(3) on behalf of a designated person, finds the thing\nfor which the designated person or person was searching, the\ndesignated person may seize the thing.\nDesignated person may seize a dangerous thing\n(2) If, as a result of conducting a search under section 14J, a\ndesignated person, or a person who conducted the search under\nsubsection 14J(3) on behalf of a designated person, finds a weapon,\nor other thing, that the designated person suspects on reasonable\ngrounds is likely to be used by the suspect or another to cause\ndeath or serious harm to a person:\n\n\nConstitution, functions and powers of the Australian Federal Police  Part II\nPowers and duties of members, special members and protective service officers relating\nto protective service functions  Division 4\nSection 14L\nAustralian Federal Police Act 1979\nCompilation date: 08/11/2025\n(a) in respect of whom the Australian Federal Police is\nperforming protective service functions; or\n(b) who is in a place, or in the vicinity of a person, place or\nthing, in respect of which the Australian Federal Police is\nperforming protective service functions;\nthe designated person may seize the weapon or thing.\nProtective service officer must deliver thing seized to police officer\n(3) If the designated person is a protective service officer, the\ndesignated person must ensure that the thing is delivered into the\ncustody of a police officer as soon as practicable."
    },
