    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-14l",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "14L How seized things must be dealt with",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-14l"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf, p.49",
      "values:originalText": "Seizure notice to be served\n(1) A police officer who is for the time being responsible for a thing\nseized under section 14K must, within 7 days after the seizure day,\nserve a seizure notice on:\n(a) the owner of the thing; or\n(b) if the owner of the thing cannot be identified after reasonable\ninquiries—the person from whom the thing was seized.\n(2) Subsection (1) does not apply if:\n(a) both:\n(i) the owner of the thing cannot be identified after\nreasonable inquiries; and\n(ii) the thing was not seized from a person; or\n(b) it is not possible to serve the person required to be served\nunder subsection (1).\n(3) A seizure notice must:\n(a) identify the thing; and\n(b) state the date on which the thing was seized; and\n(c) state the ground or grounds on which the thing was seized;\nand",
      "skos:definition": "Seizure notice to be served\n(1) A police officer who is for the time being responsible for a thing\nseized under section 14K must, within 7 days after the seizure day,\nserve a seizure notice on:\n(a) the owner of the thing; or\n(b) if the owner of the thing cannot be identified after reasonable\ninquiries—the person from whom the thing was seized.\n(2) Subsection (1) does not apply if:\n(a) both:\n(i) the owner of the thing cannot be identified after\nreasonable inquiries; and\n(ii) the thing was not seized from a person; or\n(b) it is not possible to serve the person required to be served\nunder subsection (1).\n(3) A seizure notice must:\n(a) identify the thing; and\n(b) state the date on which the thing was seized; and\n(c) state the ground or grounds on which the thing was seized;\nand",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-14l-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-14l-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-14l-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "0da13b6e3e0319356b5ff773083704ef5d8ba92e58327022548903343c25a340"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-14l",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "14L How seized things must be dealt with",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 49,
      "values:originalText": "Seizure notice to be served\n(1) A police officer who is for the time being responsible for a thing\nseized under section 14K must, within 7 days after the seizure day,\nserve a seizure notice on:\n(a) the owner of the thing; or\n(b) if the owner of the thing cannot be identified after reasonable\ninquiries—the person from whom the thing was seized.\n(2) Subsection (1) does not apply if:\n(a) both:\n(i) the owner of the thing cannot be identified after\nreasonable inquiries; and\n(ii) the thing was not seized from a person; or\n(b) it is not possible to serve the person required to be served\nunder subsection (1).\n(3) A seizure notice must:\n(a) identify the thing; and\n(b) state the date on which the thing was seized; and\n(c) state the ground or grounds on which the thing was seized;\nand"
    },
