    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-22",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "22 Termination of appointment",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-22"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf, p.57",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Governor-General may terminate the appointment of the\nCommissioner or a Deputy Commissioner by reason of\nmisbehaviour or physical or mental incapacity.\n(2) If the Commissioner or a Deputy Commissioner:\n(a) engages in paid employment outside the duties of his or her\noffice without the approval of the Minister;\n(b) is absent from duty, except on leave of absence under\nsection 21, for 14 consecutive days, or for 28 days in any 12\nmonths; or\n(c) becomes bankrupt or applies to take the benefit of any law\nfor the relief of bankrupt or insolvent debtors, compounds\nwith his or her creditors or makes an assignment of his or her\nremuneration for their benefit;\nthe Governor-General shall terminate his or her appointment.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The Governor-General may terminate the appointment of the\nCommissioner or a Deputy Commissioner by reason of\nmisbehaviour or physical or mental incapacity.\n(2) If the Commissioner or a Deputy Commissioner:\n(a) engages in paid employment outside the duties of his or her\noffice without the approval of the Minister;\n(b) is absent from duty, except on leave of absence under\nsection 21, for 14 consecutive days, or for 28 days in any 12\nmonths; or\n(c) becomes bankrupt or applies to take the benefit of any law\nfor the relief of bankrupt or insolvent debtors, compounds\nwith his or her creditors or makes an assignment of his or her\nremuneration for their benefit;\nthe Governor-General shall terminate his or her appointment.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-22-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-22-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "4b345410e6d505fd41e3a9b6aac68de2a4a96feb0fd5e919a40b8b00a145b916"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-22",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "22 Termination of appointment",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 57,
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Governor-General may terminate the appointment of the\nCommissioner or a Deputy Commissioner by reason of\nmisbehaviour or physical or mental incapacity.\n(2) If the Commissioner or a Deputy Commissioner:\n(a) engages in paid employment outside the duties of his or her\noffice without the approval of the Minister;\n(b) is absent from duty, except on leave of absence under\nsection 21, for 14 consecutive days, or for 28 days in any 12\nmonths; or\n(c) becomes bankrupt or applies to take the benefit of any law\nfor the relief of bankrupt or insolvent debtors, compounds\nwith his or her creditors or makes an assignment of his or her\nremuneration for their benefit;\nthe Governor-General shall terminate his or her appointment."
    },
