    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-42d",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "42D Property of a person",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-42d"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf, p.152",
      "values:originalText": "(1) For the purpose of making a restraining order against the property\nof a person, that property is to be taken to include property in\nwhich the person has a beneficial interest.\n(2) For the purpose of making a restraining order against the property\nof a person who is a defendant within the meaning of Division 3A,\nthat property is to be taken to include:\n(a) any property of the person that has been transferred by way\nof gift to another person within 2 years immediately before\nthe day on which the restraining order is made or the day on\n\n\nLoss of certain superannuation rights and benefits  Part VA\nIntroductory  Division 1\nSection 42E\nAustralian Federal Police Act 1979\nCompilation date: 08/11/2025\nwhich the person is charged with the relevant offence,\nwhichever is the first to happen; or\n(b) where property of the person has been transferred to another\nperson within that period for a consideration whose value is\nless than the commercial value of the property when the\ntransfer was made—an interest in the property equal to the\nproportion worked out using the formula:\nwhere:\nVP (value of the property) is the number of dollars in the\ncommercial value of the property when the transfer was\nmade.\nVC (value of consideration) is the number of dollars in the\nvalue of the consideration for the transfer when the transfer\nwas made.\n(3) Property that is to be taken to be property of a person under\nsubsection (1) is available to satisfy a recovery order made against\nthe person, whether or not a restraining order has been made\nagainst the property.\n(4) Property that is to be taken to be property of a person under\nsubsection (2) is available to satisfy a recovery order made against\nthe person if, and only if, a restraining order has been made against\nthe property.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) For the purpose of making a restraining order against the property\nof a person, that property is to be taken to include property in\nwhich the person has a beneficial interest.\n(2) For the purpose of making a restraining order against the property\nof a person who is a defendant within the meaning of Division 3A,\nthat property is to be taken to include:\n(a) any property of the person that has been transferred by way\nof gift to another person within 2 years immediately before\nthe day on which the restraining order is made or the day on\n\n\nLoss of certain superannuation rights and benefits  Part VA\nIntroductory  Division 1\nSection 42E\nAustralian Federal Police Act 1979\nCompilation date: 08/11/2025\nwhich the person is charged with the relevant offence,\nwhichever is the first to happen; or\n(b) where property of the person has been transferred to another\nperson within that period for a consideration whose value is\nless than the commercial value of the property when the\ntransfer was made—an interest in the property equal to the\nproportion worked out using the formula:\nwhere:\nVP (value of the property) is the number of dollars in the\ncommercial value of the property when the transfer was\nmade.\nVC (value of consideration) is the number of dollars in the\nvalue of the consideration for the transfer when the transfer\nwas made.\n(3) Property that is to be taken to be property of a person under\nsubsection (1) is available to satisfy a recovery order made against\nthe person, whether or not a restraining order has been made\nagainst the property.\n(4) Property that is to be taken to be property of a person under\nsubsection (2) is available to satisfy a recovery order made against\nthe person if, and only if, a restraining order has been made against\nthe property.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-42d-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-42d-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-42d-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-42d-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "db17d4dfebcf724fe04d8af74bccdea79afbec0510b0d97232cf0c36c3a95b4c"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-42d",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "42D Property of a person",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 152,
      "values:originalText": "(1) For the purpose of making a restraining order against the property\nof a person, that property is to be taken to include property in\nwhich the person has a beneficial interest.\n(2) For the purpose of making a restraining order against the property\nof a person who is a defendant within the meaning of Division 3A,\nthat property is to be taken to include:\n(a) any property of the person that has been transferred by way\nof gift to another person within 2 years immediately before\nthe day on which the restraining order is made or the day on\n\n\nLoss of certain superannuation rights and benefits  Part VA\nIntroductory  Division 1\nSection 42E\nAustralian Federal Police Act 1979\nCompilation date: 08/11/2025\nwhich the person is charged with the relevant offence,\nwhichever is the first to happen; or\n(b) where property of the person has been transferred to another\nperson within that period for a consideration whose value is\nless than the commercial value of the property when the\ntransfer was made—an interest in the property equal to the\nproportion worked out using the formula:\nwhere:\nVP (value of the property) is the number of dollars in the\ncommercial value of the property when the transfer was\nmade.\nVC (value of consideration) is the number of dollars in the\nvalue of the consideration for the transfer when the transfer\nwas made.\n(3) Property that is to be taken to be property of a person under\nsubsection (1) is available to satisfy a recovery order made against\nthe person, whether or not a restraining order has been made\nagainst the property.\n(4) Property that is to be taken to be property of a person under\nsubsection (2) is available to satisfy a recovery order made against\nthe person if, and only if, a restraining order has been made against\nthe property."
    },
