    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-42f",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "42F Effective control of property",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-42f"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf, p.154",
      "values:originalText": "(1) Property may be subject to the effective control of a person within\nthe meaning of this Part, whether or not the person has an interest\nin the property.\n(2) Without limiting the generality of any other provision of this Part,\nin determining:\n(a) whether or not property is subject to the effective control of a\nperson; or\n(b) whether or not there are reasonable grounds to believe that\nproperty is subject to the effective control of a person;\nregard may be had to:\n(c) shareholdings in, debentures over or directorships of a\ncompany that has an interest (whether direct or indirect) in\nthe property; and\n(d) a trust that has a relationship to the property; and\n(e) family, domestic and business relationships between persons\nhaving an interest in the property, or in companies of the kind\nmentioned in paragraph (c) or trusts of the kind mentioned in\nparagraph (d), and other persons.\n(3) For the purposes of paragraph (2)(e), family relationships are taken\nto include the following (without limitation):\n(a) relationships between de facto partners (within the meaning\nof the Acts Interpretation Act 1901);\n(b) relationships of child and parent that arise if someone is the\nchild of a person because of the definition of child in this\nsection;\n(c) relationships traced through relationships mentioned in\nparagraphs (a) and (b).\n(4) In this section:\n\n\nLoss of certain superannuation rights and benefits  Part VA\nIntroductory  Division 1\nSection 42G\nAustralian Federal Police Act 1979\nCompilation date: 08/11/2025\nchild: without limiting who is a child of a person for the purposes\nof this Act, someone is the child of a person if he or she is a child\nof the person within the meaning of the Family Law Act 1975.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) Property may be subject to the effective control of a person within\nthe meaning of this Part, whether or not the person has an interest\nin the property.\n(2) Without limiting the generality of any other provision of this Part,\nin determining:\n(a) whether or not property is subject to the effective control of a\nperson; or\n(b) whether or not there are reasonable grounds to believe that\nproperty is subject to the effective control of a person;\nregard may be had to:\n(c) shareholdings in, debentures over or directorships of a\ncompany that has an interest (whether direct or indirect) in\nthe property; and\n(d) a trust that has a relationship to the property; and\n(e) family, domestic and business relationships between persons\nhaving an interest in the property, or in companies of the kind\nmentioned in paragraph (c) or trusts of the kind mentioned in\nparagraph (d), and other persons.\n(3) For the purposes of paragraph (2)(e), family relationships are taken\nto include the following (without limitation):\n(a) relationships between de facto partners (within the meaning\nof the Acts Interpretation Act 1901);\n(b) relationships of child and parent that arise if someone is the\nchild of a person because of the definition of child in this\nsection;\n(c) relationships traced through relationships mentioned in\nparagraphs (a) and (b).\n(4) In this section:\n\n\nLoss of certain superannuation rights and benefits  Part VA\nIntroductory  Division 1\nSection 42G\nAustralian Federal Police Act 1979\nCompilation date: 08/11/2025\nchild: without limiting who is a child of a person for the purposes\nof this Act, someone is the child of a person if he or she is a child\nof the person within the meaning of the Family Law Act 1975.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-42f-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-42f-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-42f-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-42f-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "69431f08d5867c03efcc9c79ea2717d86cc487f69f1d28575857962468f3c84a"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-42f",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "42F Effective control of property",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 154,
      "values:originalText": "(1) Property may be subject to the effective control of a person within\nthe meaning of this Part, whether or not the person has an interest\nin the property.\n(2) Without limiting the generality of any other provision of this Part,\nin determining:\n(a) whether or not property is subject to the effective control of a\nperson; or\n(b) whether or not there are reasonable grounds to believe that\nproperty is subject to the effective control of a person;\nregard may be had to:\n(c) shareholdings in, debentures over or directorships of a\ncompany that has an interest (whether direct or indirect) in\nthe property; and\n(d) a trust that has a relationship to the property; and\n(e) family, domestic and business relationships between persons\nhaving an interest in the property, or in companies of the kind\nmentioned in paragraph (c) or trusts of the kind mentioned in\nparagraph (d), and other persons.\n(3) For the purposes of paragraph (2)(e), family relationships are taken\nto include the following (without limitation):\n(a) relationships between de facto partners (within the meaning\nof the Acts Interpretation Act 1901);\n(b) relationships of child and parent that arise if someone is the\nchild of a person because of the definition of child in this\nsection;\n(c) relationships traced through relationships mentioned in\nparagraphs (a) and (b).\n(4) In this section:\n\n\nLoss of certain superannuation rights and benefits  Part VA\nIntroductory  Division 1\nSection 42G\nAustralian Federal Police Act 1979\nCompilation date: 08/11/2025\nchild: without limiting who is a child of a person for the purposes\nof this Act, someone is the child of a person if he or she is a child\nof the person within the meaning of the Family Law Act 1975."
    },
