    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-46",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "46 Superannuation orders",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-46"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf, p.159",
      "values:originalText": "(1) Subject to subsection (2), where an application is made to an\nappropriate court for a superannuation order in respect of a person,\nthe court shall, if satisfied that the offence to which the application\nrelates is a corruption offence, by order, declare that:\n(a) the person was convicted of a corruption offence; and\n(b) this Part applies in relation to:\n(i) the rights of, and benefits paid or payable to or in\nrespect of, the person under any superannuation scheme;\nand\n(ii) any entitlement of the person to receive an adjustment\npayment.\n(2) Where a person is, because of paragraph 42C(1)(b), to be taken to\nhave been convicted of an offence, a court must not make a\nsuperannuation order in relation to the person unless the court is\nalso satisfied, on the balance of probabilities, that the person has\nabsconded and:\n(a) the person has been committed for trial for the offence; or\n(b) the court is satisfied, having regard to all the evidence before\nit, that a reasonable jury, properly instructed, could lawfully\nfind the person guilty of the offence.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) Subject to subsection (2), where an application is made to an\nappropriate court for a superannuation order in respect of a person,\nthe court shall, if satisfied that the offence to which the application\nrelates is a corruption offence, by order, declare that:\n(a) the person was convicted of a corruption offence; and\n(b) this Part applies in relation to:\n(i) the rights of, and benefits paid or payable to or in\nrespect of, the person under any superannuation scheme;\nand\n(ii) any entitlement of the person to receive an adjustment\npayment.\n(2) Where a person is, because of paragraph 42C(1)(b), to be taken to\nhave been convicted of an offence, a court must not make a\nsuperannuation order in relation to the person unless the court is\nalso satisfied, on the balance of probabilities, that the person has\nabsconded and:\n(a) the person has been committed for trial for the offence; or\n(b) the court is satisfied, having regard to all the evidence before\nit, that a reasonable jury, properly instructed, could lawfully\nfind the person guilty of the offence.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-46-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-46-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "f35f1fefa0392aaa24a22a3c1d30657e2eb262890820846c7bfe603c1b8a92b2"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-46",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "46 Superannuation orders",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 159,
      "values:originalText": "(1) Subject to subsection (2), where an application is made to an\nappropriate court for a superannuation order in respect of a person,\nthe court shall, if satisfied that the offence to which the application\nrelates is a corruption offence, by order, declare that:\n(a) the person was convicted of a corruption offence; and\n(b) this Part applies in relation to:\n(i) the rights of, and benefits paid or payable to or in\nrespect of, the person under any superannuation scheme;\nand\n(ii) any entitlement of the person to receive an adjustment\npayment.\n(2) Where a person is, because of paragraph 42C(1)(b), to be taken to\nhave been convicted of an offence, a court must not make a\nsuperannuation order in relation to the person unless the court is\nalso satisfied, on the balance of probabilities, that the person has\nabsconded and:\n(a) the person has been committed for trial for the offence; or\n(b) the court is satisfied, having regard to all the evidence before\nit, that a reasonable jury, properly instructed, could lawfully\nfind the person guilty of the offence."
    },
