    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-49c",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "49C Restraining order may be subject to conditions",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-49c"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf, p.167",
      "values:originalText": "(1) A restraining order against a person’s property may be made\nsubject to such conditions as the court thinks fit, for example,\nconditions providing for meeting, out of the property or a specified\npart of the property, all or any of the following:\n(a) the person’s reasonable living expenses (including the\nreasonable living expenses of any dependants of the person)\nand reasonable business expenses;\n(b) the person’s taxed legal expenses in defending a criminal\ncharge;\n(c) a specified debt incurred by the person in good faith (being a\ndebt to which neither paragraph (a) or (b) applies).",
      "skos:definition": "(1) A restraining order against a person’s property may be made\nsubject to such conditions as the court thinks fit, for example,\nconditions providing for meeting, out of the property or a specified\npart of the property, all or any of the following:\n(a) the person’s reasonable living expenses (including the\nreasonable living expenses of any dependants of the person)\nand reasonable business expenses;\n(b) the person’s taxed legal expenses in defending a criminal\ncharge;\n(c) a specified debt incurred by the person in good faith (being a\ndebt to which neither paragraph (a) or (b) applies).",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-49c-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-49c-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "63282c8348c4f9da4cb78e29cb87280e8f5d13932f217b941b22347e7390931d",
      "skos:note": "Restraining order conditions for a person's property"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-49c",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "49C Restraining order may be subject to conditions",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 167,
      "values:originalText": "(1) A restraining order against a person’s property may be made\nsubject to such conditions as the court thinks fit, for example,\nconditions providing for meeting, out of the property or a specified\npart of the property, all or any of the following:\n(a) the person’s reasonable living expenses (including the\nreasonable living expenses of any dependants of the person)\nand reasonable business expenses;\n(b) the person’s taxed legal expenses in defending a criminal\ncharge;\n(c) a specified debt incurred by the person in good faith (being a\ndebt to which neither paragraph (a) or (b) applies)."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-49c-norm",
      "@type": "values:Obligation",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-49c"
