    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-49j",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "49J Ancillary orders",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-49j"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf, p.171",
      "values:originalText": "(1) Where an appropriate court makes a restraining order, it may, at\nthe same time or at any later time, make any ancillary orders that it\nthinks appropriate, for example:\n(a) an order varying the property to which the restraining order\nrelates;\n(b) an order varying any condition to which the restraining order\nis subject; and\n(c) an order for the examination on oath before the court or the\nRegistrar of the court of any person, including:\n(i) the owner of property subject to the restraining order (in\nthis paragraph called the owner); or\n(ii) the defendant;\nabout the affairs (including the nature and location of any\nproperty) of:\n(iii) anyone else who is either the owner or the defendant, or\nboth; and\n(iv) if the person to be examined is either the owner or the\ndefendant, or both—that person; and\n(d) an order about carrying out any undertaking with respect to\nthe payment of damages or costs given by the\nCommonwealth in connection with the making of the\nrestraining order.\n(2) An ancillary order may be made on application by:\n(a) the DPP; or\n(b) the owner of property subject to the restraining order; or\n(c) with the leave of the court—any other person.\n(3) Where:\n(a) a defendant has been convicted of, or has been charged or is\nabout to be charged with, an offence; and\n(b) an appropriate court makes a restraining order against\nproperty in reliance on the conviction, charging or proposed\ncharging; and",
      "skos:definition": "(1) Where an appropriate court makes a restraining order, it may, at\nthe same time or at any later time, make any ancillary orders that it\nthinks appropriate, for example:\n(a) an order varying the property to which the restraining order\nrelates;\n(b) an order varying any condition to which the restraining order\nis subject; and\n(c) an order for the examination on oath before the court or the\nRegistrar of the court of any person, including:\n(i) the owner of property subject to the restraining order (in\nthis paragraph called the owner); or\n(ii) the defendant;\nabout the affairs (including the nature and location of any\nproperty) of:\n(iii) anyone else who is either the owner or the defendant, or\nboth; and\n(iv) if the person to be examined is either the owner or the\ndefendant, or both—that person; and\n(d) an order about carrying out any undertaking with respect to\nthe payment of damages or costs given by the\nCommonwealth in connection with the making of the\nrestraining order.\n(2) An ancillary order may be made on application by:\n(a) the DPP; or\n(b) the owner of property subject to the restraining order; or\n(c) with the leave of the court—any other person.\n(3) Where:\n(a) a defendant has been convicted of, or has been charged or is\nabout to be charged with, an offence; and\n(b) an appropriate court makes a restraining order against\nproperty in reliance on the conviction, charging or proposed\ncharging; and",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-49j-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-49j-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-49j-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "a1af248f9d4531f22afb7ae60873251ba1f3e4c0ea88595ea3a7362b5bfea6d7"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-49j",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "49J Ancillary orders",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 171,
      "values:originalText": "(1) Where an appropriate court makes a restraining order, it may, at\nthe same time or at any later time, make any ancillary orders that it\nthinks appropriate, for example:\n(a) an order varying the property to which the restraining order\nrelates;\n(b) an order varying any condition to which the restraining order\nis subject; and\n(c) an order for the examination on oath before the court or the\nRegistrar of the court of any person, including:\n(i) the owner of property subject to the restraining order (in\nthis paragraph called the owner); or\n(ii) the defendant;\nabout the affairs (including the nature and location of any\nproperty) of:\n(iii) anyone else who is either the owner or the defendant, or\nboth; and\n(iv) if the person to be examined is either the owner or the\ndefendant, or both—that person; and\n(d) an order about carrying out any undertaking with respect to\nthe payment of damages or costs given by the\nCommonwealth in connection with the making of the\nrestraining order.\n(2) An ancillary order may be made on application by:\n(a) the DPP; or\n(b) the owner of property subject to the restraining order; or\n(c) with the leave of the court—any other person.\n(3) Where:\n(a) a defendant has been convicted of, or has been charged or is\nabout to be charged with, an offence; and\n(b) an appropriate court makes a restraining order against\nproperty in reliance on the conviction, charging or proposed\ncharging; and"
    },
