    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-49n",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "49N Restraining orders: revocation",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-49n"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf, p.175",
      "values:originalText": "Where a court has made a restraining order against a person’s\nproperty, the court may, on application made by the person, revoke\nthe order if:\n(a) where the order was made in reliance on the person’s\nconviction of an offence or the charging, or proposed\ncharging, of the person with an offence—the person gives\nsecurity satisfactory to the court for the payment of any\namount that may be specified in a recovery order against the\nperson; or\n(b) the person gives undertakings satisfactory to the court\nconcerning the person’s property.\n49P  When do restraining orders cease to have effect?\n(1) A restraining order ceases to have effect if any of the following\nthings happen:\n(a) the defendant is acquitted of the offence, or the charge is\nwithdrawn or otherwise disposed of without conviction and\nthe defendant has not been charged with a related corruption\noffence before the acquittal, withdrawal or disposal;",
      "skos:definition": "Where a court has made a restraining order against a person’s\nproperty, the court may, on application made by the person, revoke\nthe order if:\n(a) where the order was made in reliance on the person’s\nconviction of an offence or the charging, or proposed\ncharging, of the person with an offence—the person gives\nsecurity satisfactory to the court for the payment of any\namount that may be specified in a recovery order against the\nperson; or\n(b) the person gives undertakings satisfactory to the court\nconcerning the person’s property.\n49P  When do restraining orders cease to have effect?\n(1) A restraining order ceases to have effect if any of the following\nthings happen:\n(a) the defendant is acquitted of the offence, or the charge is\nwithdrawn or otherwise disposed of without conviction and\nthe defendant has not been charged with a related corruption\noffence before the acquittal, withdrawal or disposal;",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-49n-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-49n-logic-deontic-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-49n-logic-alleninterval-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-49n-logic-ltl-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-49n-logic-diffusion-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-49n-logic-cogai-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "44976578b9533a7839e7f0a84eb317b5975e15f3534de028ef7f6b600cc8e0d9",
      "skos:note": "Security or undertakings may be required to revoke a restraining order."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-49n",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "49N Restraining orders: revocation",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 175,
      "values:originalText": "Where a court has made a restraining order against a person’s\nproperty, the court may, on application made by the person, revoke\nthe order if:\n(a) where the order was made in reliance on the person’s\nconviction of an offence or the charging, or proposed\ncharging, of the person with an offence—the person gives\nsecurity satisfactory to the court for the payment of any\namount that may be specified in a recovery order against the\nperson; or\n(b) the person gives undertakings satisfactory to the court\nconcerning the person’s property.\n49P  When do restraining orders cease to have effect?\n(1) A restraining order ceases to have effect if any of the following\nthings happen:\n(a) the defendant is acquitted of the offence, or the charge is\nwithdrawn or otherwise disposed of without conviction and\nthe defendant has not been charged with a related corruption\noffence before the acquittal, withdrawal or disposal;"
    },
