    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-64",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "64 Assault etc.",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-64"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf, p.187",
      "values:originalText": "The court convicting a person of an offence against section 147.1\nor 149.1 of the Criminal Code that relates to a member, special\nmember, protective service officer or special protective service\nofficer may, order the offender to pay such compensation as the\ncourt thinks fit for any damage or injury caused by the offender to\nthe uniform, clothing or accoutrements of the member, special\nmember, protective service officer or special protective service\nofficer concerned or for any medical or other expenses incurred in\nconsequence of personal injury sustained by him or her by reason\nof the offence.",
      "skos:definition": "The court convicting a person of an offence against section 147.1\nor 149.1 of the Criminal Code that relates to a member, special\nmember, protective service officer or special protective service\nofficer may, order the offender to pay such compensation as the\ncourt thinks fit for any damage or injury caused by the offender to\nthe uniform, clothing or accoutrements of the member, special\nmember, protective service officer or special protective service\nofficer concerned or for any medical or other expenses incurred in\nconsequence of personal injury sustained by him or her by reason\nof the offence.",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-64-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-64-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "ad62ddebdfc3559246207e54fef7574d699b441c3735d5aa9df393546f58e347",
      "skos:note": "Offender must pay compensation for damage to uniform or accoutrements."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-64",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "64 Assault etc.",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 187,
      "values:originalText": "The court convicting a person of an offence against section 147.1\nor 149.1 of the Criminal Code that relates to a member, special\nmember, protective service officer or special protective service\nofficer may, order the offender to pay such compensation as the\ncourt thinks fit for any damage or injury caused by the offender to\nthe uniform, clothing or accoutrements of the member, special\nmember, protective service officer or special protective service\nofficer concerned or for any medical or other expenses incurred in\nconsequence of personal injury sustained by him or her by reason\nof the offence."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-64-norm",
      "@type": "values:Obligation",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-64"
      },
      "values:deonticStatus": {
        "@id": "values:HeuristicDerived"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "values:borneBy": {
        "@id": "values:State"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": "borne by: State"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-64-logic-deontic-1",
      "@type": "cml:LogicApplication",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "cml:operator": "obligation",
      "cml:logicSummary": "Obligation to pay compensation.",
      "cml:executionSurface": "modalities::logic::deontic",
      "cml:confidence": {
        "@value": 1.0,
        "@type": "xsd:decimal"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-64a",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "64A Members in uniform to wear identification numbers",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-64a"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf, p.188",
      "values:originalText": "(1) A member must, at all times when he or she is wearing his or her\npolice uniform, wear his or her identification number clearly\nvisible on the uniform.\nPenalty: 5 penalty units.\n(1A) Subsection (1) is an offence of strict liability.\nNote:\nFor strict liability, see section 6.1 of the Criminal Code.\n(2) It is a defence to a prosecution for an offence against subsection (1)\nif the defendant proves that his or her identification number was\nnot clearly visible on his or her uniform in consequence of:\n(a) the act of another person (not being a member) done without\nthe consent of the defendant; or\n(b) an unintentional omission on the part of the defendant.\n(3) The Commissioner shall take such steps as are necessary to enable\nmembers to whom subsection (1) applies to comply with that\nsubsection.\n64AAA  Protective service officers in uniform to wear identification\nnumbers\n(1) A protective service officer must, at all times when he or she is\nwearing his or her protective service officer uniform, wear his or\nher identification number clearly visible on the uniform.\nPenalty: 5 penalty units.\n(2) Subsection (1) is an offence of strict liability.\nNote:\nFor strict liability, see section 6.1 of the Criminal Code.\n(3) It is a defence to a prosecution for an offence against subsection (1)\nif the defendant proves that his or her identification number was\nnot clearly visible on the uniform as a consequence of:\n(a) the act of another person (not being a protective service\nofficer) done without the consent of the defendant; or\n\n\nMiscellaneous  Part VI\n\nSection 64AA\nAustralian Federal Police Act 1979\nCompilation date: 08/11/2025\n(b) an unintentional omission of the defendant.\nNote:\nThe defendant bears a legal burden in relation to the matter in\nsubsection (3). See section 13.4 of the Criminal Code.\n(4) The Commissioner must take such steps as are necessary to enable\nprotective service officers to whom subsection (1) applies to\ncomply with that subsection.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) A member must, at all times when he or she is wearing his or her\npolice uniform, wear his or her identification number clearly\nvisible on the uniform.\nPenalty: 5 penalty units.\n(1A) Subsection (1) is an offence of strict liability.\nNote:\nFor strict liability, see section 6.1 of the Criminal Code.\n(2) It is a defence to a prosecution for an offence against subsection (1)\nif the defendant proves that his or her identification number was\nnot clearly visible on his or her uniform in consequence of:\n(a) the act of another person (not being a member) done without\nthe consent of the defendant; or\n(b) an unintentional omission on the part of the defendant.\n(3) The Commissioner shall take such steps as are necessary to enable\nmembers to whom subsection (1) applies to comply with that\nsubsection.\n64AAA  Protective service officers in uniform to wear identification\nnumbers\n(1) A protective service officer must, at all times when he or she is\nwearing his or her protective service officer uniform, wear his or\nher identification number clearly visible on the uniform.\nPenalty: 5 penalty units.\n(2) Subsection (1) is an offence of strict liability.\nNote:\nFor strict liability, see section 6.1 of the Criminal Code.\n(3) It is a defence to a prosecution for an offence against subsection (1)\nif the defendant proves that his or her identification number was\nnot clearly visible on the uniform as a consequence of:\n(a) the act of another person (not being a protective service\nofficer) done without the consent of the defendant; or\n\n\nMiscellaneous  Part VI\n\nSection 64AA\nAustralian Federal Police Act 1979\nCompilation date: 08/11/2025\n(b) an unintentional omission of the defendant.\nNote:\nThe defendant bears a legal burden in relation to the matter in\nsubsection (3). See section 13.4 of the Criminal Code.\n(4) The Commissioner must take such steps as are necessary to enable\nprotective service officers to whom subsection (1) applies to\ncomply with that subsection.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-64a-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-64a-ss-1a"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-64a-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-64a-ss-3"
        },
