    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-64a-ss-3",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "64A(3) Members in uniform to wear identification numbers",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-64a-ss-3"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf, p.188",
      "values:originalText": "(3) It is a defence to a prosecution for an offence against subsection (1)\nif the defendant proves that his or her identification number was\nnot clearly visible on the uniform as a consequence of:\n(a) the act of another person (not being a protective service\nofficer) done without the consent of the defendant; or\n\n\nMiscellaneous  Part VI\n\nSection 64AA\nAustralian Federal Police Act 1979\nCompilation date: 08/11/2025\n(b) an unintentional omission of the defendant.\nNote:\nThe defendant bears a legal burden in relation to the matter in\nsubsection (3). See section 13.4 of the Criminal Code.",
      "skos:definition": "(3) It is a defence to a prosecution for an offence against subsection (1)\nif the defendant proves that his or her identification number was\nnot clearly visible on the uniform as a consequence of:\n(a) the act of another person (not being a protective service\nofficer) done without the consent of the defendant; or\n\n\nMiscellaneous  Part VI\n\nSection 64AA\nAustralian Federal Police Act 1979\nCompilation date: 08/11/2025\n(b) an unintentional omission of the defendant.\nNote:\nThe defendant bears a legal burden in relation to the matter in\nsubsection (3). See section 13.4 of the Criminal Code.",
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-64a"
      },
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-64a-ss-3-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-64a-ss-3-logic-deontic-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-64a-ss-3-logic-deontic-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "0322f578505e3f00324756cdb29db31ecd5081072dd06f928ae7ae6236a04ef4",
      "skos:note": "Defence against prosecution for offence under subsection (1) The Commissioner must take steps to enable members to comply with subsection (1)."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-federal-police-act-1979#sec-64a-ss-3",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "64A(3) Members in uniform to wear identification numbers",
      "values:kind": "subsection",
      "cof:pageNumber": 188,
      "values:originalText": "(3) It is a defence to a prosecution for an offence against subsection (1)\nif the defendant proves that his or her identification number was\nnot clearly visible on the uniform as a consequence of:\n(a) the act of another person (not being a protective service\nofficer) done without the consent of the defendant; or\n\n\nMiscellaneous  Part VI\n\nSection 64AA\nAustralian Federal Police Act 1979\nCompilation date: 08/11/2025\n(b) an unintentional omission of the defendant.\nNote:\nThe defendant bears a legal burden in relation to the matter in\nsubsection (3). See section 13.4 of the Criminal Code."
    },
